      "id": "vblA",
      "code_hash": "990a9e0f179739e0f4fe283543f745eb",
      "outputs": [
        {
          "type": "data",
          "data": {
            "text/markdown": "<span class=\"markdown prose dark:prose-invert contents\"><h1 id=\"11-engines-and-sql\">11. Engines and SQL</h1>\n<span class=\"paragraph\">Say your pipeline was proved and tested on DuckDB on your laptop, and\nthe platform team wants it on Spark next quarter. The proof hasn't\nchanged, but will the answers?</span>\n<span class=\"paragraph\">A proof is about the Lean model. Whether an engine <em>computes</em> what the\nmodel says is a separate question, and engines disagree with each other\nmore than you'd think: about NULL join keys, NaN, integer overflow, the\nrounding of integer division, the order of ties. So plans in <code>sverify</code>\ncome from one producer (the vendored Ibis producer, or DataFusion's for\nSQL strings), but they can <strong>run</strong> on three engines in process\n(<code>sverify.engines.ENGINES</code>), and on Spark:</span>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">engine</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">how the plan runs</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\"><code>\"duckdb\"</code> (default)</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">the SQL generator (<code>sverify.engines.to_sql</code>) writes the plan as DuckDB SQL</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\"><code>\"polars\"</code></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">a translator turns the plan into a Polars <code>LazyFrame</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\"><code>\"datafusion\"</code></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">DataFusion's own Substrait consumer (the <code>sverify[datafusion]</code> extra)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\"><code>\"spark\"</code>, or a Spark session</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">the SQL generator writes Spark SQL (the <code>sverify[spark]</code> extra; below)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<span class=\"paragraph\">Polars and DuckDB run <strong>Lean's written form</strong> of the plan\n(<code>sverify.engines.written_plan</code>, chapter 14), so what runs is exactly\nwhat was checked. Their translators\ndon't take engine defaults: they spell out the model's semantics, so\nthat disagreements with Lean show up as test failures instead of quiet\ndifferences.</span>\n<h2 id=\"the-same-plan-on-three-engines\">The same plan on three engines</h2>\n<span class=\"paragraph\">We'll line them up. Pick a query; each engine runs the same checked\nplan (<code>sverify.engines.run(con, plan, engine)</code>), next to Lean's\nreference interpreter:</span></span>"
          }
        }
      ],
