    {
      "id": "SFPL",
      "code_hash": "c045b0d71657414b29afcd848cfec29a",
      "outputs": [
        {
          "type": "data",
          "data": {
            "text/markdown": "<span class=\"markdown prose dark:prose-invert contents\"><span class=\"paragraph\">What to look for:</span>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>NULL join keys</strong> never match (<code>NULL = NULL</code> is not TRUE), on every\n  engine, because each translator says so explicitly.</li>\n<li><strong>Integer division truncates</strong> toward zero, as in DataFusion and Lean\n  (<code>-7 / 2 = -3</code>). Polars' own <code>//</code> floors (<code>-4</code>); the translator uses\n  truncation instead.</li>\n<li><strong>NaN</strong> is one value, equal to itself and above every number, so\n  <code>NaN &gt; 0</code> is TRUE; and \u22120.0 is 0.0 (chapter 12), on every engine.</li>\n<li><strong><code>qty + 1</code> on the largest integer</strong> is where engines really differ:\n  DataFusion silently wraps around to the smallest integer, while the\n  Polars and DuckDB translators <strong>raise</strong>, because Lean's integers are\n  unbounded and a wrapped result is not the model's answer. That's the\n  \"may wrap around\" warning of chapter 6, made visible.</li>\n</ul>\n<span class=\"paragraph\"><strong>Lean's column</strong> is missing for the last two queries. Values in the\nmodel are untyped: a double is stored as a very large integer code\n(<code>SubstraitLean/Float.lean</code>), so the checker's printer can't tell a\ndouble from an integer beyond 64 bits, and shows neither faithfully.\nThe test suite compares doubles through the engines instead.</span>\n<h2 id=\"choosing-the-engine-for-a-node\">Choosing the engine for a node</h2>\n<span class=\"paragraph\">A node (chapters 9 and 10) doesn't fix its engine when you build it:\nthe plan is the same everywhere, and you pick where it runs each time\nyou refresh, with <code>node.refresh(engine=\"polars\")</code>, <code>\"datafusion\"</code>,\n<code>\"spark\"</code> or a Spark session (DuckDB in memory is the default). We'll\nbuild the NULL-key join as a transform over <code>t</code> and <code>u</code> and refresh a\nfresh node on each engine:</span></span>"
          }
        }
      ],
      "console": []
    },
