            "text/markdown": "<span class=\"markdown prose dark:prose-invert contents\"><span class=\"paragraph\">Long, but every line is there for a reason. Try\n<code>select k, row_number() over (partition by k order by qty) as rn from t</code>\nin each dialect: DuckDB gets an ordinal column so ties break the way\nLean's stable sort breaks them, while <strong>Snowflake and Spark refuse</strong>,\nbecause their tables have no row order to break ties with. If a\ncontract report says <code>deterministic</code> (chapter 7), pass <code>tie_free=True</code>\nto promise there are no ties.</span>\n<span class=\"paragraph\">Snowflake-specific choices: integers are <code>NUMBER(38,0)</code> checked against\nthe 64-bit range, timestamps <code>TIMESTAMP_NTZ(6)</code>, intervals counts of\nmicroseconds (Snowflake has no interval type), every identifier quoted.\nWhere Snowflake can't match the model, the query raises or is refused\ninstead of answering differently, as for an interval-valued result\ncolumn.</span>\n<h2 id=\"spark-and-databricks\">Spark and Databricks</h2>\n<span class=\"paragraph\">The Spark dialect (<code>sverify.engines.SPARK</code>) targets Spark 4 and\nDatabricks SQL; it is also what <code>node.refresh(engine=\"spark\")</code> runs. It\nassumes the session settings of <code>sverify.engines.SPARK_CONF</code>, above all\n<code>spark.sql.ansi.enabled</code>: without ANSI mode an overflow wraps around and\na bad cast is NULL. Spark answers several of\n<a href=\"?file=17_backend_semantics.py\">chapter 17</a>'s questions its own way, and\neach is spelled out or refused:</span>\n<ul>\n<li><code>/</code> of integers is a double, so division is <code>div</code>; <code>/</code> of doubles\n  raises on a zero divisor in ANSI mode, so <code>x / 0.0</code> is written as the\n  IEEE \u00b1inf or NaN.</li>\n<li><code>CAST(9.3e18 AS BIGINT)</code> saturates instead of raising, and <code>FLOOR</code>\n  and <code>CEIL</code> return integers: each is checked or repaired.</li>\n<li><code>SUM</code> of <code>BIGINT</code> raises on an overflowing partial sum, though Lean's\n  total may fit: it adds <code>DECIMAL(38,0)</code> instead.</li>\n<li><code>TIMESTAMP</code> is the session time zone's (<code>TIMESTAMP_LTZ</code>), and so is\n  what <code>date_trunc</code> returns: the SQL stays in <code>TIMESTAMP_NTZ</code>.</li>\n<li><code>POWER</code> of doubles is Java's <code>Math.pow</code> (within 1 ulp of C's), so it\n  is refused, as are sorts and windows over a table without\n  <code>tie_free=True</code>. Over <code>VALUES</code>, a <code>SUM</code> of doubles adds in Lean's\n  order (<code>aggregate</code> over the sorted list).</li>\n</ul>\n<span class=\"paragraph\">Unlike Snowflake, Spark runs locally: the tests run every coverage case\nand every query of the Snowflake suite on a local Spark session and\ncompare them with <code>Rel.eval</code>. DataFusion's own departures from the model\nare in <a href=\"?file=16_datafusion_semantics.py\">chapter 16</a>.</span>\n<h2 id=\"testing-the-engines-against-the-model\">Testing the engines against the model</h2>\n<span class=\"paragraph\">Moving a pipeline to another engine is only safe if every engine gives\nthe model's answer. The Lean evaluator is the specification. The test\nsuite runs every fixture and every update plan on DataFusion, Polars and\nDuckDB with random tables and compares them with each other and with\n<code>Rel.eval</code>, row for row in Lean's order where order matters (<code>LIMIT</code>,\nwindows). The Snowflake SQL is checked by parsing and by running it on\n<a href=\"https://github.com/tekumara/fakesnow\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">fakesnow</a>, a Snowflake emulator,\nor on a real account if you configure one.</span>\n<span class=\"paragraph\">You can now run a checked plan, or refresh a node, on DuckDB, Polars,\nDataFusion or Spark, or hand it to Snowflake as SQL, and you know where an engine could\ndepart from the model: the translators and the SQL generator spell out\nthe model's semantics and raise or refuse rather than answer\ndifferently, and the tests compare the engines with <code>Rel.eval</code>. That\nends the tour of what you can build. Chapter 12,\n<a href=\"?file=12_values.py\">Doubles, time and text</a>, starts the reference and\ndeep-dive part, with how the model treats the values themselves.</span></span>"
          }
        }
      ],
